# Frames for routing (frames.ath is kept separate). See ontology/SOURCES.md and routing/BASE.md.
check "python3 -I routing/check.py"
frame "Addison: map-service routing on OpenStreetMap architecture for the outer buses (ontology/SOURCES.md)" reading "Dijkstra, A* (halved exact and one-landmark ALT heuristics) and contraction hierarchies return the exact distance on every connected graph up to 5 vertices with weights 1..3 (106932 graphs)" -> routing.ath label checked-finite reach 2 cost 120
frame "Addison: planned routes against haphazard buses (transit/BASE.md; routing/BASE.md)" reading "max hitting time / hop distance over connected n-stop graphs is n^2-3n+3 for n=2..5, the clique with one pendant stop" -> routing.ath label checked-finite reach 2 cost 80
frame "Addison: the centre as a road network (routing/BASE.md)" reading "programs linked by shared evidence files: 14 components among 17 programs; most programs are unreachable from the rest" -> 2 label checked-finite reach 2 cost 80
# Stays open: Google's production routing is not public; nothing here describes it.
frame "Addison: the Google Maps algorithm (routing/BASE.md)" reading "the method Google Maps uses in production" -> none label open reach 1 cost 160
record routing routing checked-finite "frames_routing.ath: routing/check.py passes. On all 106932 connected graphs on 2..5 vertices with edge weights in {1,2,3}, Dijkstra, A* (halved exact distance; one-landmark ALT) and contraction hierarchies (fixed order; every order for n<=4) give the Floyd-Warshall distance for every pair. A* with the halved heuristic never settles more nodes than Dijkstra; ALT can, on ties (12431 queries). Max hitting time over hop distance is n^2-3n+3 for n=2..5."
